1.1 Plan for this lecture
All code in these notes, and in the homework, uses PyTorch [1]. This lecture covers the three parts of PyTorch that everything else is built on, on the handwritten digits from the course overview:

how PyTorch stores the arrays that hold a network’s inputs, parameters and outputs;
how PyTorch computes the derivative of a loss from the code that computes the loss;
how PyTorch packages a model and the loop that fits it.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
The syllabus covers how the course runs. This lecture is about what the course is for:

why hand-designed features run out, shown on a dataset of handwritten digits;
what representation learning and deep learning are;
where deep learning sits within machine learning and artificial intelligence;
a short history of neural networks;
the topics and tools of these notes.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
Deep learning reuses the pieces of supervised learning from DATA 371 and DATA 471, which lds covers in full. This lecture reviews three of them on the handwritten digits from the course overview, then shows where each one reappears in deep learning:

the parts of a learning problem: a set of candidate rules, the parameters that pick one out, and a loss function;
why models are trained on a smooth loss rather than on their error rate;
why a model is judged on data it was not fit to.
